<div class="task-intro"><div><span class="eyebrow">Support and useful tools</span><p>Find the right service or recover from a missing file or access problem.</p></div></div><div class="choice-grid"><a class="choice-card" href="$link.page('CRH', 'Canto DAM Access')"><span class="choice-index" aria-hidden="true">01</span><h2>Find photos or video</h2><p>Search and download from the staff media library.</p><span class="choice-go">Get to Canto <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></span></a><a class="choice-card" href="$link.page('CRH', 'Submitting a Request')"><span class="choice-index" aria-hidden="true">02</span><h2>Request creative work</h2><p>Brief new work or ask for a review.</p><span class="choice-go">Open the request guide <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></span></a><a class="choice-card" href="https://jira.skatelescope.org/servicedesk/customer/portal/364"><span class="choice-index" aria-hidden="true">03</span><h2>A missing or broken resource</h2><p>Send the exact page link and what happened.</p><span class="choice-go">Report a problem ↗ <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></span></a></div><details class="guidance-detail"><summary>Where should I look for presentations?</summary><div><p>Start with <a href="$link.page('CRH', 'Slide Decks & Presentations')" class="">presentation resources</a>. Released decks belong in the Hub. A page description is not a file download.</p></div></details><details class="guidance-detail"><summary>Why can I browse but not upload?</summary><div><p>Browsing, downloading and contributing can have different permissions. Ask for the <a href="$link.page('CRH', 'How To Submit Your Deck')" class="">appropriate contribution route</a> rather than treating a library link as an upload form.</p></div></details><details class="guidance-detail"><summary>Is my request booked once submitted?</summary><div><p>No. The team confirms scope, capacity and delivery timing after review. <a href="$link.page('CRH', 'Turnaround Times')" class="">Read how timing is agreed</a>.</p></div></details><section class="reading-section"><h2>Practical guides</h2><div class="resource-links"><a href="$link.page('CRH', 'Accessibility Guidelines')" class="">Accessibility checks</a><a href="$link.page('CRH', 'Font Downloads')" class="">Fonts</a><a href="$link.page('CRH', 'Preparing Files for Print')" class="">Preparing print files</a><a href="$link.page('CRH', 'Events Support')" class="">Event materials</a><a href="$link.page('CRH', 'Adobe Firefly')" class="">AI image tools</a></div></section>
#elseif($page.id.toString() == "381891873")
<div class="task-intro"><div><span class="eyebrow">Make the work usable</span><p>Build accessibility into the document or presentation, then check the exported result.</p></div></div><section class="reading-section"><h2>Content and structure</h2><ul><li>Use clear headings, descriptive links and a logical reading order.</li><li>Give informative images alternative text that conveys their purpose. Decorative images should be ignored by assistive technology, for example with empty alt text in HTML.</li><li>Caption video and provide an appropriate text alternative for audio.</li></ul></section><section class="reading-section"><h2>Appearance and interaction</h2><ul><li>Use readable contrast: WCAG’s minimum is generally 4.5:1 for normal text and 3:1 for qualifying large text.</li><li>Do not rely on colour alone. Use labels, symbols or patterns too.</li><li>Check keyboard access and focus visibility for interactive content.</li></ul></section><aside class="next-step"><div><h2>Check the relevant standard</h2><p>These checks are a starting point, not a claim that the work passes a full accessibility audit.</p></div><a href="https://www.w3.org/WAI/WCAG22/quickref/" class="text-link">Open the W3C reference ↗</a></aside>
